[{"id":"VRCE-2-94-1","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":1,"english":"Then Rāma cast his eyes on (Bharata) as clad in bark and wearing matted locks he lay on the earth with joined hands, incapable of being gazed at, like the Sun at the time of the universal dissolution. Then recognizing him a little, he took by the hand his brother Bharata, lean, with a pallid countenance. And smelling the crown of his head, and embracing that descendant of Raghu, Rāma took Bharata on his lap and asked him affectionately.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.742,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094001","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"eb4c228c9744e0c2339fc83da7bdf0772acf11f4995633e515b2eb4d43fd8614","compact":"d58b3414dd772d462dad75ae869ea27c32ff080592ce4d4f40f637e6244e2314","nasal":"e7c3a9ef427c9954d13dc43f646a2d6e7ce49e66a24b17ddf7eb085ae8ee6b5e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-2","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":2,"english":"Where was your father, child, that you have come to the forest? It certainly behoves you not to come to the forest while he is living.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094002","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3349cfc41316042a0dd1a39dc40f4aba5cb7477ab6be5047a799a73802b4aec7","compact":"3df18395f853b0d47ae28ce207aca20e329b941a358ecfca0a623bf4a637125c","nasal":"78b28f4d87cd2f65365e131b2eb41c97cf3cd4865839ee8973e54d7cd6e94bc9"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-3","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":3,"english":"Ah! I see you come from far after a long time. Why, my child, have you come to this gloomy forest?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094003","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0f396217e74481a6200fd75dc02ecbcefc27bc553801dcab20765c1bfd8ba636","compact":"5dba30284cb3ba0a585b9256450a4d010c50db5edd8e21f4c373d25aa5aee9ad","nasal":"5dba30284cb3ba0a585b9256450a4d010c50db5edd8e21f4c373d25aa5aee9ad"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-4","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":4,"english":"Is that truthful one, that performer of Rajasüya and Aśvamedha, ever devoted to righteousness, king Dasaratha, well?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.965,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094004","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c77e7cf2cd3d17435b4db629ba0b3a1c5bfc60fd63933b12ecf73598722ec1c1","compact":"a70d9c8543a38e15cf588984c0f92e8d354db794f54d799c5b5bca538083455c","nasal":"a70d9c8543a38e15cf588984c0f92e8d354db794f54d799c5b5bca538083455c"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-5","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":5,"english":"My child, is that exceedingly effulgent and learned Brāhmaṇa ever steady in morality, the priest of the Iksvākus, duly honoured?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094005","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f03bbeca9c0c2ce06cd2230f99f7fa73a523f0e366c5e8d8b5bf3032dc04da08","compact":"825e5863d486adadb8122747642f7874f75038ef1e24110aab6e425cc5bf0f80","nasal":"c4bc79de272f012e8140884142f25645d34504d0eb505c9e6a4f95f69baed7e2"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-6","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":6,"english":"And, my child, are Kausalyā, and Sumitrā having a son, in happiness? And is the noble Kaikeyi in spirits?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.967,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094006","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2dd3da6647112254b90ea65749a207afd283598e1e4e9362faacb33146dc4b80","compact":"43cea0fbd2028e294d2312ddf39a036e0cebdcc2aac3533ac2104bc0f609bd18","nasal":"cba5bb786c520278b336ec49986138daa8bc23b5326f717ae5e93e1e0be223d7"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-7","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":7,"english":"And is that one, sprung from a mighty line, humble and versed in various lore, your priest, who performs every ceremony, who bears no ill will, and whose gaze is ever fixed upon our welfare, honoured?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094007","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6b0341c7e58f2d216733260fddff798cb3de6ea8ba9530be4ba05aec3f71a1f0","compact":"99306d774b6635db53359c9ad3bf026ef8a2fd52d937777db27b73b1efc1fc03","nasal":"cc5f2dcfef683a8dad56209bb52a23e897198e2508990131f0f2c65ebaab5d66"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-8","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":8,"english":"Do intelligent and sincere people cognizant of the rules, look after the sacrificial fire? And do they regularly inform you of the proper seasons for performing the fire-sacrifice?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094008","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d740083b91dcb9cfc437161d51240db6ba1fecf3f9bf000ffc6c9d36d0e249d9","compact":"f8a80e76b76d7779930fcd98f119b0e745c3c4b9bb8d253e5bc0823968cbf64b","nasal":"f8a80e76b76d7779930fcd98f119b0e745c3c4b9bb8d253e5bc0823968cbf64b"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-9","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":9,"english":"And do not disregard the preceptor Sudhanvā versed in excellent arms whether inspired with mantras or not, and accomplished in the knowledge of polity?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.881,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094009","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6abe5ac6b98f1e20fd068e3b47592003108616d2a69bd14706c52aa5c4518ecc","compact":"5a693427dbaa21e6cdef47769759beaaba8c81d1b8a13ff1190df708ea659256","nasal":"7c08d9c6d0b1bf1ed2b3a6f7cd43ddd9762f4acfa05b9a1153bd57596f175e14"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-10","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":10,"english":"And, my child, have you employed as your controlled, well-born, and understanding signs, who are like your own self?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094010","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"35a4dfef6da94df851be69666374204d6cab2d5728656ba1fa7e58e0c8681bfe","compact":"24ee80fc446d947a8977b5de03c85e4afc1b2c1f7b7e632f38d105cb6fb7fb5e","nasal":"fcd9901775e1b6e571237f63497ee167e490d734a5d503d1dfd42af13774d380"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-94-11","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":94,"verse":11,"english":"O descendant of Raghu, counsel well kept by clever councillors versed in lore, is the root of victory with kings.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.766,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-100-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2094011","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"8d65f5c07fd614d2d75e91754bf8098c73e0b407baf22463c1dcbe93135a6f8d","compact":"5207f969b009cfa80299b0f7dfe2bace9963b2d72a6be577f842b7f23dd64267","nasal":"93b81c3